  nonce: 3a9c  # 3a9c
  message: "NOT A DEFECT. FALSE ALARM, AND THE FAULT IS THE INSTRUMENT'S INPUT -- MINE.\n\nTHE CORPUS HAS TWO ROOTS HOLDING THE SAME REPO AT DIFFERENT COMMITS:\n    ~/repos/alertmanager               710984e  2026-02-25\n    ~/whole_bunch_of_repos/alertmanager 4781bbe  2026-01-09\nWI-joluk's 2026-08-06 baseline measured ~/repos/. I measured\n~/whole_bunch_of_repos/, because LIVE.md names that one as \"the corpus\" and\ndoes not mention the other. Same script, same grammar, same pipeline --\ndifferent checkout.\n\nTHE DISCRIMINATOR, run to a byte. The ORIGINAL unmodified instrument against\n~/repos/alertmanager at dev 85fa799fc4:\n    walkable 1329  forfeit 445  share 33.5%  unwalk 1251  sites 1255\nwhich is forfeit_share.log's recorded row EXACTLY. And on the full re-run all\nfour third-party pairs reproduce: 1329/33.5%, 4773/35.5%, 1747/26.9%,\n1512/27.8%. There is no drift in DDG edge production. Nothing regressed.\n\nWHAT I HAD ALREADY RULED OUT BEFORE FINDING IT, recorded so the next person\ndoes not repeat the search: not the instrument (original reproduced my\nnumbers on the wrong root), not the corpus HEAD (static since January), not\nthe grammar (tree_sitter_go 0.25.0 installed 2026-02-04, predating the\nbaseline), not any of the six cfg.py / cfg_nodes commits (caching, docstrings\nand pure additions; none touches CFG construction), not a swallowed exception\n(instrumented: 0 raised, 0 bailed out), and not the ddg_build explicit-stack\nrewrite (the instrument enumerates with its own walk). Every one of those was\na real check and every one came back clean, which is what kept the search\ngoing past the point where the answer was \"wrong input\".\n\nTHE LESSON, which is the only durable thing here: A MEASUREMENT THAT DOES NOT\nNAME ITS CORPUS ROOT IS NOT REPRODUCIBLE. forfeit_share.log records repo NAMES\n(\"alertmanager\"), not paths, so two runs against two different checkouts\nproduce two comparable-looking tables and no way to tell them apart. The\nwidened instrument now takes full paths on the command line and they are\nrecorded with the numbers.\n\nClosing wont_do rather than done: there was nothing to fix."  # 3a9c
